      "side": 1,
      "message": "Marc Zyngier left the following comment on the LMKL\n\n\u003e This is the *return value*, right? Not the function number. How can\nyou tell about the 64-bitness of the function number based on that?\n\nLink: https://lore.kernel.org/all/20250619-virtio-msg-ffa-v5-0-412c98558807@google.com/T/#t\n\nLooking at this closer. I don\u0027t think it is always the case that `a0` contains a function ID. For instance, `FFA_VERSION` puts the negotiated version or `FFA_RET_NOT_SUPPORTED` in that register.\n\nI figured your concern was with the messaging interfaces where the response to a 64-bit request (e.g. `FFA_MSG_SEND_DIRECT_REQ2`) can be either 64 (e.g. `FFA_MSG_SEND_DIRECT_RESP2`) or 32 bit (`FFA_ERROR`) but it is not clear to me that it would be a problem to look at the function ID of the request and thus send an 18-register reply no matter what the result of the interface is (e.g. `FFA_ERROR`). Maybe your concern is something else, can you enlighten me?",
